\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Granger precedence is not causation.}
Granger causality tests predictive improvement, not true causation~\cite{granger1969investigating,shojaie2022granger}: an unobserved process could independently drive both token entropy and diversity metrics.
Our central claim is scoped accordingly---``token entropy Granger-precedes diversity collapse''---and bivariate tests cannot exclude confounders; multivariate VAR models would be required to test for confounders directly.

\paragraph{Practical utility paradox.}
The framework works best where it is least needed: at $\alpha{\geq}0.75$, PermG reliably detects canary precedence, but degradation is already severe; at $\alpha{\leq}0.45$, where early warning would be most valuable, detection remains null.
However, in realistic pipelines, contamination $\alpha$ is unknown and typically increases over time as web-scraped corpora accumulate machine-generated text.
A continuous monitoring dashboard would trigger when $\alpha$ crosses the detectability threshold, converting the static limitation into a temporal advantage.
The regime map itself is actionable: practitioners who know $\alpha{<}0.25$ should invest in data provenance rather than metric monitoring.

\paragraph{Four-layer detection taxonomy.}
Our results suggest a layered taxonomy ordered by onset threshold:
(1)~distributional dimensionality (effective rank, onset $\alpha{\approx}0.10$);
(2)~distributional instability (entropy variance, suggestive at $\alpha{\approx}0.25$);
(3)~causal coupling (Granger-type, onset $\alpha_c{\approx}0.50$); and
(4)~dynamical stability (AC1, suggestive but not conclusive at $T{=}11$).
The 40-percentage-point gap between structural distortion (Layer~1) and causal evidence (Layer~3) constitutes the sharp detection onset.
Layers~1 and~3 are established; Layers~2 and~4 require longer generation protocols ($T{\geq}50$; \citealt{dakos2012methods}).

\paragraph{Synchronous collapse at $\alpha{=}0.60$.}
The non-monotonic dip at $\alpha{=}0.60$ (PermG fwd below both $\alpha{=}0.50$ and $\alpha{=}0.75$) is consistent with the $S \times R$ prediction (\S\ref{sec:theory}): relaxation time matching drives $R \to 0$, so entropy and diversity respond within the same generation despite rising signal strength.
This creates a four-regime structure---blind, onset, synchronous dip, detection---rather than the binary boundary assumed by simpler models.

\paragraph{Method recommendation.}
The primary value of the multi-method framework is the systematic comparison it enables.
By applying five methods with different inductive biases, the framework reveals why each succeeds or fails: naive cross-correlation inflates FPR via reverse-directional associations; differenced cross-correlation cannot separate forward from reverse; threshold onset lacks power at $T{=}11$; Toda--Yamamoto achieves moderate detection (3--4/8) but is sensitive to augmentation order.
This process of elimination converges on PermG as the recommended method: lowest null-control FPR among directionally-specific methods (11.1\%, 4/36 across 6 seeds; DiffXCorr achieves 2.8\% but cannot separate forward from reverse), consistent detection at $\alpha{\geq}0.50$, and clean directional separation (24/24 forward, 0/24 reverse at $\alpha{=}1.0$ on WikiText).

\paragraph{Conclusion.}
Lead-lag analysis in iterative training collapse is far more fragile than assumed: method choice alone inflates spurious-signal rates to 75\% null-control FPR, first-differencing exposes canary$\to$perplexity associations as entirely spurious, and regime dependence causes the same test to shift from significant to null.
PermG identifies token entropy as a one-generation-ahead canary for diversity collapse, validated across 15 model-seed combinations spanning six families (345M--6.9B) with detection in 13 cases (87\%).
Directional asymmetry is domain-dependent (unidirectional on WikiText, bidirectional on C4), and canary-based predictive cessation reduces peak perplexity by 58\%.
Extended discussion of cross-architecture generalization, domain-dependent directionality, token entropy versus perplexity, NLP-specific constraints, and future directions appears in Appendix~\ref{app:extended_discussion}.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\paragraph{Methodological pitfalls.}
\label{sec:pitfalls}
Lead-lag analysis in iterative model collapse is fragile along three axes, each documented with data from \S\ref{sec:regime}--\ref{sec:dose_response}.
\emph{Method sensitivity}: five detection methods applied to identical data ($\alpha{=}1.0$, 16 canary$\to$downstream pairs) yield significance rates from 100\% (naive cross-correlation) to 37.5\% (permutation Granger), with inter-method agreement at $\kappa{=}0.185$ and a spurious-signal rate ${\geq}62.5\%$.
\emph{Spurious regression}: first-differencing to remove shared trends~\cite{granger1974spurious} eliminates canary$\to$perplexity associations ($F{\approx}0$, $p{=}0.99$) while preserving canary$\to$diversity (24/24$\to$24/24), exposing an entire analysis channel as trend-driven rather than temporally precedent.
\emph{Regime dependence}: the same Granger test finds 0/8 pairs significant through $\alpha{=}0.45$ but 6/8 at $\alpha{=}0.50$, a categorical shift at a 5-percentage-point boundary that a single-$\alpha$ study would miss entirely.
These pitfalls motivate systematic method comparison rather than reliance on any single detection approach.

\paragraph{Multi-Method Consensus Test (MMCT).}
\label{sec:mmct}
We apply the Multi-Method Consensus Test, a weighted consensus framework in the specification curve~\cite{simonsohn2020specification} and multi-method early-warning~\cite{scheffer2009early,bell2022modeling} traditions, adapted to the constraints of iterative training analysis: short series ($T{=}5$--$20$), non-stationarity by design, and heterogeneous method sensitivities.
MMCT is a consensus testing protocol, not a novel statistical method.
It compares five lead-lag detection methods through FPR-inverse weighted consensus, calibrated against null-control ($\alpha{=}0.0$) data where any detection is a false positive.
Each method $m_k$ receives weight $w_k = 1/\max(\hat{\text{FPR}}_k, \epsilon)$ with $\epsilon{=}0.01$; the weighted score $W(X \to Y) = \sum_k w_k d_k / \sum_k w_k$ yields formal $p$-values from the empirical null distribution (Algorithm~\ref{alg:mmct}; Appendix).
MMCT identifies permutation Granger (PermG) as the method with the lowest null-control FPR among directionally-specific methods (11.1\%, 4/36 across 6 seeds; DiffXCorr achieves 2.8\% but lacks directional separation) combined with consistent detection across contamination levels, and we recommend it as the primary detection method based on this systematic comparison.

\paragraph{Permutation Granger causality.}
PermG tests whether past values of canary metric $X$ improve prediction of downstream metric $Y$ beyond $Y$'s own history; Granger precedence establishes temporal predictive improvement, not true causality~\cite{shojaie2022granger}.
We apply 5{,}000 permutations on first-differenced series with Benjamini--Hochberg FDR correction at $q{=}0.05$ across 8 validated canary$\to$downstream pairs (2 canaries $\times$ 4 downstream metrics).
Three properties distinguish PermG: a nonparametric null distribution robust to the short non-stationary series typical of iterative training~\cite{boettiger2012limits}, built-in directionality (forward and reverse tested separately), and a hyperparameter-free test statistic.
The remaining four methods each exhibit a specific limitation: naive cross-correlation has 75\% null-control FPR; differenced cross-correlation detects bidirectionally without directional separation; threshold onset is power-limited at $T{=}11$ under corrected $3\sigma$ calibration (0/8 significant in 8 of 12 cross-architecture conditions); Toda--Yamamoto~\cite{toda1995statistical} achieves moderate supplementary detection (3--4/8 at $\alpha{\geq}0.50$) with reduced power from degree-of-freedom costs of lag augmentation.
Per-method dose-response profiles, Shapley value analysis, and the full MMCT algorithm appear in the Appendix.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_framework.pdf}
\caption{Analytical framework overview. (a)~Iterative contamination loop: each generation fine-tunes on an $\alpha$-fraction synthetic mixture, producing canary (entropy, ECE) and downstream (distinct-$n$, perplexity) time series. (b)~Multi-Method Consensus Test: five detection methods weighted by inverse null-control FPR converge to a $K{\geq}3$ consensus decision. (c)~Detection regimes in the $(\alpha, T)$ plane: the boundary $\alpha_c(T)$ separates blind and detection zones, with onset and sync-dip as transitional regions.}
\label{fig:framework}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Signal-Times-Resolution Framework}
\label{sec:theory}

We apply standard statistical power scaling to PermG under the Signal$\times$Resolution (S$\times$R) framework.
Detectability of canary$\to$downstream temporal precedence depends on two competing factors:
\begin{equation}
    \text{Detectability}(\alpha) \;\propto\; S(\alpha) \times R(\alpha),
    \label{eq:detectability}
\end{equation}
where $S(\alpha)$ is signal strength (magnitude of distributional shift, monotonically increasing with $\alpha$) and $R(\alpha)$ is temporal resolution (the generational gap between canary and downstream responses).
$R(\alpha)$ is non-monotonic: at low $\alpha$, entropy and diversity respond in different generations ($R$ large); at $\alpha{\approx}0.60$, relaxation time matching compresses both responses into a single generation ($R \to 0$); at high $\alpha$, the severity of entropy collapse outpaces the diversity cascade, restoring the generational offset.
The product $S \times R$ predicts four regimes: \emph{blind} ($\alpha \leq 0.45$; $S$ below detection floor), \emph{onset} ($\alpha \approx 0.50$; $S$ crosses threshold while $R$ remains high), \emph{synchronous dip} ($\alpha \approx 0.60$; $R \to 0$ suppresses detection despite rising $S$), and \emph{detection} ($\alpha \geq 0.75$; $S$ dominates).
Full empirical confirmation appears in \S\ref{sec:regime} and \S\ref{sec:dose_response}.

Under a permutation test framework, detection power scales as $\mathrm{Power}(\alpha, T) = \Phi\bigl(S \cdot R \cdot \sqrt{T} / \sigma_{\mathrm{noise}}\bigr)$, where $\Phi$ is the standard normal CDF and the $\sqrt{T}$ scaling follows standard power analysis.
The sharp detection onset at $\alpha_c$ (at $T{=}10$) is consistent with a power threshold: when $S \cdot R \cdot \sqrt{T}$ first exceeds the detection floor, power undergoes a steep onset from near-zero to near-one.
This generates three predictions for $T{=}20$: (i)~$\alpha_c$ left-shifts as increased $T$ raises power at all $\alpha$; (ii)~the transition smooths as $\sqrt{T}$ broadens the power curve; (iii)~different methods exhibit different $\alpha_c$ values due to method-specific power curves.
Bootstrap calibration ($N{=}10{,}000$) yields clean falsifiable tests at $\alpha{\geq}0.75$: predicted 7/8 (95\% CI: [6,8]); results below 6/8 would falsify $\sqrt{T}$ power scaling.
At $\alpha{=}0.45$, the point prediction is 0/8, but the bootstrap CI spans [0,7] reflecting $\alpha_c$ uncertainty---both detection and non-detection are informative for constraining the phase boundary location.
At $\alpha{=}0.50$, the prediction is inherently uncertain at the transition (CI: [0,7]).
Sub-threshold FDR $p{=}0.059$--$0.091$ at $\alpha{=}0.45$ is consistent with proximity to the power boundary.

\paragraph{Calibration.}
We validate error rates with built-in experimental controls.
False positive rate: at $\alpha{=}0.0$ (null control, no synthetic contamination), PermG achieves the lowest FPR among directionally-specific methods: 11.1\% across 6 seeds (4/36 pairs; Clopper--Pearson 95\% CI $[3.1\%, 26.1\%]$); under identical BH-FDR correction, DiffXCorr achieves the lowest FPR (2.8\%) but lacks forward/reverse directional separation.
False negative rate: at $\alpha{=}1.0$, all 6 effect-size reference pairs (2 canaries $\times$ 3 diversity metrics, selected by criteria independent of the detection methods) are recovered at 0\% FNR across 4 seeds.
PermG matches consensus performance at all tested contamination levels, with cross-architecture validation confirming PermG forward significance ${\geq}6/8$ in 11 of 15 conditions (\S\ref{sec:generalization}).
Full calibration details, practical guidance tiers, and method-level breakdown appear in the Appendix.

\section{Cascade Ordering}
\label{sec:cascade}

If distributional collapse follows a predictable ordering across metrics, the earliest-responding metric can serve as a canary signal.
We formalize known metric properties---entropy as a continuous indicator of mode concentration, diversity as a threshold indicator of lexical coverage---under mode amplification~\cite{shumailov2024curse,dohmatob2024tale} (derivations in Appendix~\ref{app:cascade_theory}).

\begin{designprinciple}[Entropy responds before diversity]\label{thm:entropy_leads}
Under mode amplification: \textup{(a)} entropy can decrease while the $\epsilon$-effective support $S_\epsilon(p) {=} |\{i: p_i > \epsilon\}|$ (hence distinct-$n$) is unchanged; \textup{(b)} if distinct-$n$ decreases, entropy has already decreased.
\end{designprinciple}

The asymmetry arises because entropy responds to any mass redistribution (first-order), while diversity changes only when a token probability crosses the generation threshold~$\epsilon$; the margin $\gamma = \min_{i: p_i > \epsilon}(p_i - \epsilon)$ quantifies how much shift diversity absorbs silently.

\begin{designprinciple}[Diversity lag $\geq 1$ generation]\label{prop:div_lag}
If the per-generation perturbation satisfies $\alpha\|\mathcal{T}(p_t) - p_t\|_\infty < \gamma(p_t, \epsilon)$ through entropy onset $t_H$, then diversity onset $t_D \geq t_H + 1$.
\end{designprinciple}

ECE co-moves with entropy at zero lag: peakier conditionals simultaneously decrease per-token entropy and increase confidence ($\Delta\mathrm{ECE} \approx \Delta C > 0$ whenever $\Delta\bar{H} < 0$; qualitative argument in Appendix~\ref{app:cascade_theory}).
The predicted cascade $\{H, \mathrm{ECE}\} \to \mathrm{Distinct}$-$n$ holds in 14/15 experiments at $\alpha \geq 0.30$ (GPT-2-medium s43 bidirectional; within-domain WikiText, $2\sigma$ onset threshold): entropy and ECE onset at generation~1, distinct-$n$ at generation~2 ($t_H{=}t_{\mathrm{ECE}}{=}1$, $t_D{=}2$ uniformly; \S\ref{sec:regime}; Appendix~\ref{app:moment_cascade}).
Five low-$\alpha$ experiments show no detectable onset, leaving the ordering undefined below the detection threshold.
The assumption requires monotonic entropy decline; SmolLM3-3B (entropy 0/4; Table~\ref{tab:generalization}) provides an empirical counter-example, possibly due to architectural differences in attention or normalization.
This cascade establishes entropy as the earliest available canary and explains PermG's directional selectivity: forward tests detect temporal precedence (6/8) while reverse tests find none (0/8) at $\alpha \geq 0.50$.

\section*{Ethics Statement and AI Writing Disclosure}

\paragraph{AI writing assistance.}
This paper was drafted with assistance from Claude (Anthropic), used for LaTeX formatting, literature search, and iterative revision.
All scientific claims, experimental design decisions, data analysis, and interpretations are the sole responsibility of the authors.
The AI assistant did not design experiments, run code, or make editorial judgments about which results to include or exclude.

\paragraph{Responsible NLP considerations.}
This work studies degradation in iterative language model training.
We do not release models trained on synthetic data, as they exhibit reduced diversity and calibration.
Our findings highlight a failure mode relevant to the responsible deployment of language models: unmonitored iterative training on web-crawled data (which increasingly contains LLM outputs) may degrade model quality in ways detectable only through the multi-method framework we present.
We encourage practitioners to implement canary monitoring in production training pipelines as a safeguard against silent quality degradation.

\appendix

\section{Training Details and Metric Drift}
\label{app:training_details}
\label{app:drift}

\paragraph{Hyperparameters.}
Each generation fine-tunes for 5{,}000 steps using AdamW (learning rate $5 \times 10^{-5}$, 500 warmup steps, weight decay $0.01$, batch size 4 for Pythia-410M, 32 for GPT-2-medium).
Generation uses temperature $1.0$, top-$k{=}50$, max length 512 tokens, producing 5{,}000 samples per generation from 5{,}000-sample subsets of WikiText-103.
Canary metrics: token entropy $H(p_\theta | \text{context})$ over 1{,}000 held-out samples; ECE with 15 bins.
Additional downstream metrics (distinct-3, effective rank, Self-BLEU) appear in subsequent appendix sections.

\paragraph{Metric drift.}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Metric drift (\%, generation~0$\to$10) for Pythia-410M across contamination levels. Diversity collapse occurs at all $\alpha$ levels, with the strongest decline at low-to-moderate contamination ($\alpha{=}0.25$: distinct-1 $-40.3{\pm}0.3\%$) rather than full replacement ($\alpha{=}1.0$: $-27.8{\pm}1.6\%$). $^\dagger$$\alpha{=}0.10$: single seed (seed~42, preliminary). $\alpha{=}0.00$, $\alpha{=}0.25$, $\alpha{=}0.50$, $\alpha{=}0.75$: 3-seed mean${\pm}$std (seeds 42, 43, 44); $\alpha{=}1.0$: 4-seed mean${\pm}$std (seeds 42, 43, 44, 45).}
\label{tab:drift_summary}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
$\alpha$ & Entropy & ECE & Distinct-1 & Distinct-2 & Perplexity \\
\midrule
0.00 & $-4.6{\pm}0.4$ & $+37.6{\pm}14$ & $-14.1{\pm}0.4$ & $-9.6{\pm}0.4$ & $-20.6{\pm}1.0$ \\
